{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The proposed dependence relation lacks a genuine causal mechanism: SRC_001 asserts SRC_003 'depends on' SRC_002 because SRC_002 establishes the absence of linear-cost entailment that SRC_003 needs for its defeasibility claim. But this is a logical precondition, not a substantive dependence — SRC_003's empiricist stance (that uniform pricing is an expectation, not entailment) is self-grounding precisely because defeasibility is inherent to the empiricist epistemic modality. The 'refuted if' clause tries to guard against this by conceding independence is possible, but that concession exposes the relation as non-binding: if SRC_003 can stand without any skeptical premise about underdetermination, then the supposed dependence on SRC_002's gap-noting is not a mechanism but a narrative gloss. The relation reduces to 'both note gaps in the problem text' — which the target itself admits is not a relation.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The integration relation asserts SRC_001, SRC_003, and SRC_005 share a commitment to treating the proportionality/covering principle as 'a real, defeasible assumption rather than an axiom,' with SRC_003 supplying the epistemic modality and SRC_001/SRC_005 supplying a pedagogical mechanism. But this conflates two distinct kinds of defeasibility into one 'shared mechanism': SRC_003's defeasibility is epistemic (expectation vs. entailment — a modal distinction about what follows logically), whereas SRC_001/SRC_005's 'mechanism that renders the assumption invisible by construction' is pedagogical (how the framing conceals an assumption from the solver). These are not the same mechanism — invisibility-by-construction and epistemic-modality-attribution operate on entirely different levels (curriculum design vs. logical semantics). Calling their combination an 'integration' names no causal pathway through which the pedagogical concealment and the epistemic modality reinforce each other; it merely lists them as co-occurring. The 'refuted if their mechanisms are merely topically similar without a shared commitment' clause is triggered by the target's own content, since the only shared thing is the abstract word 'defeasible' applied to different objects.","counterexample":null}]}